From ecd996ea4f436a9333db4eb46d9996f4a3fe5426 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Vis <118430440+mark-vis@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 07:08:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 24/32] (b) v2, part b-3: \caption* label: start the paragraph
 only where needed

Round-3 findings R3-L1 and R3-L2. Since c904b54d3 the label of
\caption* started the paragraph before any token it did not know (state
P, nothing removed). That made a separator macro that begins with a
space visible in vertical mode: with babel-french and pdfTeX a long
\caption* (#1\CaptionSeparator #2) started with an interword space
before the dash (2.99pt). And #1\vskip2pt #2 gave an empty line.

Now the paragraph is started only
- after removed material (as that material would have done; unchanged),
- before \\ and \newline (unchanged; they also warn), and
- before \vadjust and \@, which are errors in vertical mode (#1\@ #2 in
  vertical mode stopped with "You can't use \spacefactor in internal
  vertical mode"); they do not warn.
Other material after the label still warns once, but stays where it is:
in vertical mode a leading space of a separator macro is ignored again
(as in round 2 and as without the label) and \vskip stays vertical.

Documentation: "no paragraph if \par follows" holds only for a \par in
the group of the removed material ({\centering #1.\par}); a \par after
the end of the group ({\bfseries #1.}\par) still gives an empty line.
\relax (and \protect while typesetting) end the look-ahead without a
warning, so #1\relax\hskip1em and #1\protect\sep print the separator
silently; this is now listed with the other silent cases.

Tests: caption-interface-001 TEST 30 gets five shapes: a separator macro
with a leading space and \vskip in vertical mode (compared with the
output without the label; both warn), \@ and \vadjust in vertical mode
(no error, no warning) and \relax\hskip (printed, no warning). With the
code of the previous commit the first four differ (leading space,
14.0pt instead of 8.8pt, a \spacefactor error).
